1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,265
Push two pins into a board.

2
00:00:02,265 --> 00:00:05,987
Loop a string around them, and
pull it tight with a pencil.

3
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:10,604
Now move the pencil all the way
round, keeping the string tight.

4
00:00:10,604 --> 00:00:12,332
It draws a perfect oval.

5
00:00:12,782 --> 00:00:14,780
Diya tried it at her study desk tonight.

6
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:18,276
The pencil keeps changing
its distance from each pin.

7
00:00:18,276 --> 00:00:20,169
So why is the curve so neat?

8
00:00:20,669 --> 00:00:22,821
Because one thing never changes.

9
00:00:22,821 --> 00:00:26,534
That curve is the ellipse,
and today we find its rule.

10
00:00:27,184 --> 00:00:29,450
The two pins are called the foci.

11
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:30,915
One pin is a focus.

12
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:35,468
An ellipse is the set of all points
where the sum of the distances from

13
00:00:35,468 --> 00:00:37,277
the two foci is a constant.

14
00:00:37,677 --> 00:00:39,838
Watch the pencil P run round.

15
00:00:39,838 --> 00:00:44,119
Its distance to one focus is
in blue, to the other in gold.

16
00:00:44,519 --> 00:00:45,624
Seven and three.

17
00:00:45,624 --> 00:00:46,595
Four and six.

18
00:00:46,595 --> 00:00:48,113
Each one keeps changing.

19
00:00:48,513 --> 00:00:51,752
But add them, and you get
ten, every single time.

20
00:00:51,752 --> 00:00:53,659
That fixed sum is the string.

21
00:00:54,309 --> 00:00:55,243
Remember this.

22
00:00:55,243 --> 00:00:59,215
On an ellipse, the two distances to
the foci always add up to the same

23
00:00:59,215 --> 00:01:00,115
number.

24
00:01:00,492 --> 00:01:01,538
Now the parts.

25
00:01:01,538 --> 00:01:04,250
The midpoint between
the foci is the centre.

26
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:07,775
The long line through the
foci is the major axis.

27
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:09,337
Call its length two a.

28
00:01:09,737 --> 00:01:14,556
The short line across, through the
centre, is the minor axis, of length

29
00:01:14,556 --> 00:01:15,456
two b.

30
00:01:15,245 --> 00:01:17,160
The foci are two c apart.

31
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:20,201
Put the pencil at the
end of the major axis.

32
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:22,304
The two distances add up to two a.

33
00:01:22,304 --> 00:01:24,010
So the fixed sum is two a.

34
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:26,336
Now put it at the top.

35
00:01:26,336 --> 00:01:29,332
The two distances are
equal, so each one is a.

36
00:01:30,082 --> 00:01:34,340
That makes a right triangle with
sides b and c, and long side a.

37
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,288
So a squared equals b
squared plus c squared.

38
00:01:37,938 --> 00:01:39,246
Now the equation.

39
00:01:39,246 --> 00:01:42,464
Put the foci at minus c, zero and c, zero.

40
00:01:42,864 --> 00:01:43,764
Step one.

41
00:01:43,442 --> 00:01:45,156
The two distances add up to two a.

42
00:01:45,906 --> 00:01:46,806
Step two.

43
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:49,081
Move one root across
and square both sides.

44
00:01:49,831 --> 00:01:50,731
Step three.

45
00:01:50,699 --> 00:01:52,785
Tidy up, and square once more.

46
00:01:53,535 --> 00:01:54,435
Step four.

47
00:01:54,356 --> 00:01:57,225
Swap a squared minus c
squared for b squared.

48
00:01:57,975 --> 00:02:00,346
Divide through, and there it is.

49
00:02:00,346 --> 00:02:05,098
x squared over a squared, plus y
squared over b squared, equals one.

50
00:02:05,748 --> 00:02:08,625
One number tells you
how flat an ellipse is.

51
00:02:08,625 --> 00:02:11,049
The eccentricity, e, is c over a.

52
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:12,429
Watch e grow.

53
00:02:12,429 --> 00:02:15,550
The foci slide apart, and
the ellipse gets flatter.

54
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:18,771
For an ellipse, e always
stays between zero and one.

55
00:02:19,421 --> 00:02:20,548
Side by side.

56
00:02:20,548 --> 00:02:25,230
If the bigger number is under x
squared, the major axis lies along the

57
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:26,130
x axis.

58
00:02:26,381 --> 00:02:31,102
If the bigger number is under y
squared, the major axis stands along the

59
00:02:31,102 --> 00:02:32,002
y axis.

60
00:02:31,843 --> 00:02:34,486
The bigger number is always a squared.

61
00:02:35,086 --> 00:02:35,986
Your turn.

62
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:40,341
x squared over nine, plus y squared
over twenty five, equals one.

63
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:43,411
Which axis is major,
and where are the foci?

64
00:02:43,411 --> 00:02:44,682
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:47,882 --> 00:02:51,751
The y axis, with foci at
zero, plus or minus four!

66
00:02:51,751 --> 00:02:56,680
Twenty five is bigger, so a is
five, b is three, and c is four.

67
00:02:57,330 --> 00:02:59,020
Now, one worked example.

68
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:04,095
For four x squared plus nine y squared
equals thirty six, find a, b, c,

69
00:03:04,095 --> 00:03:06,515
the eccentricity and the latus rectum.

70
00:03:07,015 --> 00:03:07,915
Step one.

71
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:10,035
Divide everything by thirty six.

72
00:03:10,035 --> 00:03:14,114
x squared over nine, plus y
squared over four, equals one.

73
00:03:14,864 --> 00:03:15,764
Step two.

74
00:03:15,674 --> 00:03:17,730
So a is three and b is two.

75
00:03:17,730 --> 00:03:21,714
Then c is the root of nine
minus four, which is root five.

76
00:03:22,464 --> 00:03:23,364
Step three.

77
00:03:23,308 --> 00:03:25,804
The eccentricity is root five over three.

78
00:03:26,554 --> 00:03:27,454
Step four.

79
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:29,666
The latus rectum is two b squared over a.

80
00:03:29,666 --> 00:03:31,176
That is eight over three.

81
00:03:31,826 --> 00:03:33,288
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:33,638 --> 00:03:37,985
Many students write c squared equals a
squared plus b squared for an ellipse.

83
00:03:38,385 --> 00:03:42,439
But the foci sit inside the
ellipse, so c is smaller than a.

84
00:03:42,439 --> 00:03:46,129
For an ellipse, c squared is
a squared minus b squared.

85
00:03:46,779 --> 00:03:48,113
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:48,113 --> 00:03:49,034
In JEE Main.

87
00:03:49,034 --> 00:03:53,043
In January twenty twenty four, a
question gave an ellipse's foci and its

88
00:03:53,043 --> 00:03:55,110
latus rectum, and built on them.

89
00:03:55,710 --> 00:03:56,833
Exam level now.

90
00:03:56,833 --> 00:04:01,255
An ellipse has foci at plus or minus
four, zero, and every point on it

91
00:04:01,255 --> 00:04:04,203
has distances to the
foci that add up to ten.

92
00:04:04,203 --> 00:04:05,715
Find its latus rectum.

93
00:04:05,715 --> 00:04:06,885
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:12,285 --> 00:04:13,728
The answer is B.

95
00:04:13,728 --> 00:04:15,272
Eighteen over five.

96
00:04:15,772 --> 00:04:19,899
The sum is two a, so two
a is ten, and a is five.

97
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:22,117
The foci give c equals four.

98
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:25,290
b squared is a squared minus c squared.

99
00:04:25,290 --> 00:04:27,595
Twenty five minus sixteen is nine.

100
00:04:28,095 --> 00:04:30,702
The latus rectum is two b squared over a.

101
00:04:30,702 --> 00:04:33,584
Two times nine over five
is eighteen over five.

102
00:04:34,184 --> 00:04:35,724
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:35,724 --> 00:04:37,306
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:40,506 --> 00:04:42,688
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:43,088 --> 00:04:43,988
One.

106
00:04:43,503 --> 00:04:46,229
What stays the same for
every point on an ellipse?

107
00:04:47,129 --> 00:04:48,029
Two.

108
00:04:47,662 --> 00:04:52,552
For x squared over sixteen, plus y
squared over seven, equals one, find

109
00:04:52,552 --> 00:04:53,452
c.

110
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:54,781
Three.

111
00:04:54,445 --> 00:04:58,013
Why is the eccentricity of an
ellipse always less than one?

112
00:04:59,113 --> 00:05:02,542
Next time: a lamp throws a
curved shadow on the wall.

113
00:05:02,542 --> 00:05:04,670
It is a different kind of curve.

114
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:05,672
The hyperbola.
